-- SQL Datatypes.. defining the types of values that can be stored in a column.
-- 1. [Char] -> string(0-255) can store characters of fixed length ; ex-char(50)
-- 2. [Varchar] -> string(0-255) can store characters up to given length. ; ex-varchar(50)
-- varchar is dynamic and uses less memory space than char datatype...
-- 3. [Blob] -> string(0-65535), can store binary large object. ; ex-blob(100) It is used to store binary objects
-- 4. [Int] -> integer INT SIGNED(−2,147,483,648 - 2,147,483,647) 
-- The INT data type occupies 4 bytes (32 bits). 
-- Exam based point- INT in MySQL stores integers from −2³¹ to 2³¹−1 when signed. UNSIGNED removes the negative range, giving 0 to 2³²−1.
-- We can even find unsigned data range by - just taking both minimum and maximum data range & adding both and in unsigned minimum value is always 0.
-- 5. [Tinyint] -> integer (-128 - 127)
-- 6. [Bigint] -> integer (−9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807)
-- 7. [Bit] -> can store x-bit values, x can range from 1 to 64. ex - bit(2)
-- 8. [Float] -> Decimal/Point numbers - with precision of 23 digits
-- 9. [Double] -> Decimal/Point numbers - with 24 to 53 digits precision
-- 10.[Boolean] -> Boolean values 0 or 1. 
-- 11.[Date] -> date in format YYYY-MM-DD ranging from 1000-01-01 to 9999-12-31.
-- 12.[Year] -> years in 4 digits format ranging from 1901 to 2155.

